WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.350
Film Courage: Have you ever optioned or bought
a screenplay?

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:05.140
Mark Heidelberger, producer: I have optioned
and bought screenplays, yeah under Treasure

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.140
Entertainment.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.200
Before I went freelance we used to all the
time and a lot of them came from our clients

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:11.200
as well.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.580
Film Courage: Can you take us through the
process?

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:22.070
Mark: So in terms of when Treasure Entertainment
when we would, part of what I liked about

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:27.520
managing clients is that you had an in-house
stable of talent that you could draw from.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:37.750
So if we had identified something in the market,
an opportunity, for some reason we wanted

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:43.410
to do a certain type of picture, I had clients
in-house I said “Hey, what about one of

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:44.410
these ideas?

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.700
Because this is something that we’re thinking
about doing and we can develop this with you

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.109
and get it to a point where it’s strong,
maybe we’ll option it from you guys?

00:00:50.109 --> 00:00:52.559
We will buy it.”

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:58.890
So not to say that we didn’t shop around
but it kind of allowed us to get exactly what

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:02.112
we wanted, working with the writer and then
getting it to the…”Okay, this is what

00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:04.370
we want, okay we’ll option it now.”

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.540
Instead of scouring the marketplace trying
to find that exact kind of thing we wanted.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:15.120
But once we identified a strong project, either
something that we came across or something

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.000
that we had a client create with our help,
the optioning process was pretty simple.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.040
I mean a lot of time we would just do a one
year option with a one year renewal period.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.260
And whatever sort of modicum depending on
the size of the film being optioned can be.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.541
We can pay anything from a dollar if they’re
a real development writer and they’re like

00:01:37.541 --> 00:01:39.230
“Look I just want to get it out there.”

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:42.190
And it’s like “Let’s just see if we
can get this thing out there.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.610
We’re putting our sweat equity into it,
so it might just be a one dollar…you have

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:47.640
to have something called consideration.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.970
There has to be something from this side going
to this side to make it legal (even if that’s

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:51.970
a dollar).

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:52.970
“Here’s a dollar, here is your script.”

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:59.750
And an option is just basically saying you
can’t sell it to anyone else during this

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:00.750
period.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:06.190
We have the exclusive right to shop it or
set it up or buy it and if we exercise the

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:11.349
option here we’ll give you $10,000, $20,000
whatever the amount is and then the option

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:16.910
is exercised, so it gives us the option to
buy it exclusively for that period.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.329
Or we might option things up to $1,000 bucks
or $2,000 bucks.

00:02:19.329 --> 00:02:22.500
I mean it just depended on the size of the
project.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.450
If it was all indie stuff, I mean they weren’t
getting $10,000 options but you know $500

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.239
or whatever it is and then that applies against
the purchase price.

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:35.670
So if you option something for $500 and you’re
going to buy the screenplay for $20,000, that

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:41.139
$500 is to be applied against the $20,000
so you pay the $19,500 is you decide to buy

00:02:41.139 --> 00:02:42.139
it.

00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:45.790
Although those deals can be structure however
you want.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:52.680
So that’s kind of how we approached it was
“Hey in house talent, awesome!”

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.969
And because we were their managers and they
saw all the sweat equity we were putting in

00:02:56.969 --> 00:02:59.830
trying to get their career off the ground
and the project, a lot of times we could option

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:05.670
the script for a lot less than if we had just
found it and they didn’t know us “Well

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:08.480
I want 3,000 for this.”

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.650
We had already built this relationship and
so it was a good system.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.379
It worked for us.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:17.439
Film Courage: What about life story rights?

00:03:17.439 --> 00:03:24.269
Mark: Yeah, I’m working on a project right
now called WALKING ON PALMETTOS with writer

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:30.379
Jim Christell and Ed Asner (like a screen
legend), good ole’ Ed.

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:37.579
And the story is based on a real life person
(Myles Richards) who is very actively involved

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:43.069
in the project as well (he’s an executive
producer) and he had met Jim many years ago

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:46.909
through a mutual friend and Jim found this
guy’s story so interesting.

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:52.769
He sat down and he interviewed him with a
tape recorder for hours, I mean maybe a couple

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:57.379
of days worth but he was like “Wow, this
guy’s story is so interesting.”

00:03:57.379 --> 00:04:01.269
And decided that wow, this is the kind of
story that would make…I think he first decided

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:08.650
on a TV show but then he decided to make it
into a script (a feature script) and I got

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:10.760
involved when Jim came to me and asked me
to do a budget.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:17.829
And it’s a fairly large picture I would
say, it’s almost maybe studio mid-level

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:18.829
kind of budget.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:25.400
And I did the budget and Jim was very happy
with the work and asked me if I would come

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:31.640
on as a producer and work with him on it and
I came on board and I asked him “Do you

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.699
have Myles’ life rights?”

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:38.860
This guy Myles is extraordinary human being.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:43.650
He was actually a smuggler and had
started when he was like 21 or something.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:49.520
He smuggled his first batch of from
Jamaica.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.580
He was working on the Playboy, his father
ran the construction company that was building

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.860
the Playboy club and he went there for the
summer to work on it and that’s where he

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:56.930
got involved in.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:00.981
He started smuggling it and his first load
when he was like 21 he made a quarter of a

00:05:00.981 --> 00:05:02.960
million dollars, this is in the 60’s!

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:03.960
Film Courage: Oh wow.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:09.150
Mark: Right, so the thing about Myles was
he never did a load the same way twice.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:15.909
He always, there was always some innovative
way and the story goes into a couple of these

00:05:15.909 --> 00:05:22.340
(so many different ways) but he sank a ship
off of the coast of Florida on purpose loaded

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:28.419
with water-sealed bags of, filed
for a permit to do a salvage operation, so

00:05:28.419 --> 00:05:33.569
the Coast Guard got to know him and they are
salvaging, bringing up parts of the boat with

00:05:33.569 --> 00:05:36.140
Marijuana in it and the Coast Guard is right
there and they had no idea.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:41.760
I mean he came with these ingenious…so it
took the gov 15 years to even know who he

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.259
was and another 5 years to catch him.

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:50.750
And it’s very much CATCH ME IF YOU CAN,
GOODFELLAS (tonally) like the likable criminal

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:54.430
that you follow over…WOLF OF WALL STREET
that you follow them over 20 years of their

00:05:54.430 --> 00:06:00.230
life, that kind of arc and their comeuppance
and the sort of trajectory.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.090
Miles did go to prison, he was there for 11
years, he was in solitary confinement for

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:04.090
4 years.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.840
He came out a very different person and started
a legal business.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.220
He started a construction business and became
a millionaire legally.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:14.330
But it was a really interesting story and
I was like “Do you have Myles’ life rights

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:15.330
for this?”

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:19.129
And he didn’t and I said “Well, that’s
the first thing we need to do.”

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:24.060
And so we went to Myles and at that point
we had already worked with him long enough

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.729
that he knew us and trusted us.

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:28.639
I mean I had talked to him on the phone, I
had never met him personally but I talked

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:29.639
to him on the phone.

00:06:29.639 --> 00:06:37.310
Jim had been dealing with him for years and
so I had a life rights option drafted up and

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:39.130
Myles signed off on it.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:45.430
I mean we had certain agreements with him
that while we can take creative license that

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:47.169
there we certain things we wouldn’t do.

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:49.740
Myles wanted to make sure that the bad actions
were depicted realistically.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:53.280
He was like “Look, just don’t have anyone
running around with a suitcase full of a million

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:54.840
dollars that looks like this.”

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.090
He’s like “I know what a million dollars
looks like in a suitcase of $50 dollar bills

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:02.830
and it’s not like in any of these movies
where the suitcase stacked to the brim with

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:03.830
hundreds.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.150
That’s not what a million dollars looks
like.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:11.400
Just like he wanted us to stay true to a lot
of the aspects even if we took creative license

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.180
with say some of the guys that were chasing
him.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:20.599
His lawyers, in the script there is one main
lawyer that deals with his case but he’s

00:07:20.599 --> 00:07:22.469
an amalgamation of many lawyers.

00:07:22.469 --> 00:07:25.319
In real life there were many lawyers, there
wasn’t just this one guy.

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:31.280
Things like that were okay, but he wanted
to make sure the smuggling was treated accurately

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:32.750
and so we agreed to the things that he wanted.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:36.650
And so we agreed to the things that he wanted
and he signed off on that life rights agreement

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:42.699
and he really gave us creative control of
the project and the ability to take the story

00:07:42.699 --> 00:07:45.210
within those bounds (within those parameters)
where we wanted to.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:50.919
Film Courage: And so just as an option expires,
someone’s life rights expire as well and

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:54.300
then they are free to use them with another
production company?

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.300
Mark: They could.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:08:00.669
We have it so that, we have a certain term
and then it automatically renews for extended

00:08:00.669 --> 00:08:05.180
periods unless one of us cancels it so as
long as Myles is happy it’s going to keep

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:11.409
renewing unless he gives us notice within
30 days of when the expiration is.

00:08:11.409 --> 00:08:14.870
Film Courage: Fascinating and when does that
comes out?

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:20.439
Mark: Well we are working with trying to get
it off the ground for the top of 2019 in terms

00:08:20.439 --> 00:08:26.159
of production so this is a project I mentioned
that we were possibly looking at shooting

00:08:26.159 --> 00:08:27.159
in Australia.

00:08:27.159 --> 00:08:31.099
There are maybe some tax incentives we can
take advantage of plus a lot of the picture

00:08:31.099 --> 00:08:35.800
takes place in Southern California and the
Caribbean, so Australia has locations that

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:40.760
can really play for both because the Caribbean
looks very different than Southern California.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:44.750
You know, the Caribbean is very flat, white
sands, different type of palm trees, different

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.680
type of water, different type of look, the
people (it’s very different).

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:54.580
But Australia I think has options for us that
can play for both so that’s something we’re

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:55.080
looking at.

